EXT. ROOFTOP WATER TOWER - DAWN

A rusted water tower on a flat tar roof above a sleeping town. Pigeons. A pink smear of sky.

JUNE WEBER, 17, fraying denim jacket, climbs the last rung. Chewed fingernails. A paper bag in her teeth.

She hauls over the lip. Looks around.

Nobody.

She sits. Takes a donut from the bag.

A scrape behind her.

She spins.

OSCAR PELL, 17, skinny, dyed-blond hair, up the other ladder. Out of breath. Grinning.

OSCAR
Told you I'd beat you.

JUNE
You didn't.

OSCAR
I got here at five.

JUNE
Then why were you climbing?

OSCAR
I went down for coffee.

He holds up two paper cups. Hands her one. She takes it, suspicious.

JUNE
It's cold.

OSCAR
It's iced.

JUNE
It's November.

OSCAR
It's committed.

She laughs despite herself. Drinks.

Below, a milk truck rattles down Main Street. The light spreads.

A third voice, below, thin and sharp. A kid's.

KID (O.S.)
Oscar! Mom says come home!

Oscar closes his eyes.

OSCAR
Ten minutes!

KID (O.S.)
She says now!

JUNE
Who's that?

OSCAR
My sister.

JUNE
How old?

OSCAR
Nine. Terrifying.

He stands. Offers June a hand. She takes it.

JUNE
Tomorrow?

OSCAR
Same tower.

JUNE
Bring hot coffee.

OSCAR
Bring a better donut.
